# TODO - Tiered native execution engine

## Objective

Build a tiered execution engine instead of choosing between interpretation, AOT,
and JIT. Decode Malbolge into a compact execution IR, simplify that IR through
verified state-graph mathematics, compile demonstrably stable regions to native
machine code before execution, specialize hot or mutation-sensitive regions at
runtime, and deoptimize safely to the interpreter whenever a code-version guard
or speculative assumption fails. The normative VM contract remains the semantic
baseline; native tiers are accelerators of identical observable behavior.

## Acceptance

- Interpreter, AOT, JIT, native cache, and deoptimization share one observable
  VM contract; disabling every native tier produces the same guest behavior.
- Observable state, I/O, termination, and diagnostics match the declared
  semantic profile across positive, boundary, and adversarial fixtures.
- The declared scope contains no unresolved placeholder implementation or
  undocumented workaround required for this objective to function.
- Evidence is durable enough to move this TODO to `docs/todo/completed/` and
  remove the exact TODO heading without losing unfinished intent.

## Evidence

- Expected durable artifact surface: `vm/`, `execution/`, `tests/vm/`,
  `benchmarks/interpreter/`.
- Required evidence: semantic fixtures, state/I/O traces where diagnostic, and
  differential results against independent specification-conformant
  implementations; the historical interpreter is compared only on its documented
  agreement domain.
- Prerequisite completion evidence: `safe-rust-malbolge-vm`,
  `self-modification-state-graph-optimizer`.
- Active foundation: `execution/ir/main.rs` owns portable effect IR v1
  (`EffectOp`, `MemoryLiveIn`, `RegionEffectProgram`) without creating a Rust
  crate boundary. State-graph research reprojects verifier traces into this IR,
  rejects tampered profile/live-in/budget/outcome/effect fields, and preserves
  deterministic interpreter deoptimization on guard miss.
- `execution/cache/main.rs` now binds canonical byte-exact IR identity to host
  OS, ISA, backend revision, native ABI revision, and required features. Digest
  collisions never authorize reuse because full canonical/key equality remains
  authoritative; `tests/tiered_execution.rs` forces that boundary explicitly.
- Native x86-64/AArch64 code artifacts, AOT/JIT orchestration, durable cache
  storage/eviction, executable-memory policy, and end-to-end tier performance
  remain unimplemented.
